{"metadata":{"kernelspec":{"language":"python","display_name":"Python 3","name":"python3"},"language_info":{"name":"python","version":"3.12.12","mimetype":"text/x-python","codemirror_mode":{"name":"ipython","version":3},"pygments_lexer":"ipython3","nbconvert_exporter":"python","file_extension":".py"},"kaggle":{"accelerator":"none","dataSources":[{"sourceType":"competition","sourceId":125197,"databundleVersionId":15805622}],"dockerImageVersionId":31286,"isInternetEnabled":true,"language":"python","sourceType":"notebook","isGpuEnabled":false}},"nbformat_minor":4,"nbformat":4,"cells":[{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 1. Problem Understanding\n\n#### What is Customer Churn?\n**Churn** means a customer stops using a company’s service. For a telecom company, churn happens when a subscriber cancels their phone or internet plan.\n\n#### Why Do Companies Care About Churn?\nAcquiring a new customer costs **5–7× more** than retaining an existing one. If a company can identify customers who are *about to leave*, it can offer them discounts, better plans, or improved support - and keep them.\n\n#### What Is Machine Learning Doing Here?\nWe have historical data about thousands of customers: their demographics, the services they use, how long they have been customers, and - crucially - whether they eventually churned. A machine-learning model learns **patterns** in this data and then predicts the churn probability for new customers whose outcome we don’t yet know.\n\n#### What Does the Final Prediction Represent?\nWe are **not** predicting a simple Yes/No. We are predicting a **probability** between 0 and 1:\n- 0.05 → the model is quite confident the customer will stay\n- 0.92 → the model thinks the customer is very likely to churn\n\n#### Evaluation Metric - ROC-AUC\n**ROC-AUC** (Area Under the Receiver Operating Characteristic Curve) measures how well the model separates churners from non-churners *across all probability thresholds*.\n- A score of **0.5** means the model is no better than a coin flip.\n- A score of **1.0** means perfect separation.\n\nROC-AUC is preferred over plain accuracy because the dataset may be **imbalanced** (far more non-churners than churners). A model that always predicts “No churn” could get 73% accuracy but be useless - ROC-AUC catches this.\n\n---","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 2. Environment Setup and Imports\n\nI begin by importing all necessary libraries and configuring the notebook environment. I fix random seeds globally to ensure full reproducibility - any stochastic component in model training, data splitting, or encoding must produce identical results across runs.\n\nI also configure display options and suppress warnings to keep the notebook clean and readable. Memory efficiency will be important given the size of synthetic competition datasets, so I import gc for garbage collection.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"import pandas as pd\nimport numpy as np\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport seaborn as sns\nimport warnings\n\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split, cross_val_score\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import LabelEncoder, StandardScaler\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\nfrom sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestClassifier, GradientBoostingClassifier\nfrom sklearn.metrics import roc_auc_score, confusion_matrix, roc_curve, classification_report\n\n# XGBoost - install with: pip install xgboost\ntry:\n    from xgboost import XGBClassifier\n    XGBOOST_AVAILABLE = True\n    print(\"XGBoost is available.\")\nexcept ImportError:\n    XGBOOST_AVAILABLE = False\n    print(\"XGBoost not installed - we will skip it.\")\n\nwarnings.filterwarnings(\"ignore\")\nsns.set_style(\"whitegrid\")\nprint(\"All libraries loaded successfully!\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-09T13:53:06.021203Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-09T13:53:06.021453Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-09T13:53:06.027815Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-09T13:53:06.021435Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-09T13:53:06.026980Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Global Configuration \n\n# Set a fixed random seed for reproducibility\nSEED = 42  \n# Number of folds for cross-validation (splitting data into train/test multiple times)\nN_FOLDS = 5  \n# Target variable (the column we want to predict)\nTARGET = 'Churn'  \n# Unique identifier column in my dataset\nID_COL = 'id'  \n\n# Early stopping rounds for models like XGBoost/LightGBM\n# Training stops if the validation metric doesn't improve for this many rounds\nEARLY_STOPPING_ROUNDS = 100  \n\n# Number of trials for hyperparameter optimization using Optuna\nOPTUNA_TRIALS = 50  \n\n# Set random seed and Pandas display options \n\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport seaborn as sns\n\n# Fix NumPy's random generator for reproducibility\nnp.random.seed(SEED)\n\n# Configure Pandas to display more columns and rows\npd.set_option('display.max_columns', 100)  # Show up to 100 columns in outputs\npd.set_option('display.max_rows', 100)     # Show up to 100 rows in outputs\npd.set_option('display.float_format', '{:.4f}'.format)  # Format floats to 4 decimals\n\n# Set plot styles \n\n# Use Seaborn's \"whitegrid\" style for all Matplotlib plots\nplt.style.use('seaborn-v0_8-whitegrid')  \n\n# Set Seaborn color palette to \"husl\" (good for distinct categorical colors)\nsns.set_palette('husl')  \n\n#  Confirmation prints \nprint(\"All libraries imported successfully.\")\nprint(f\"Random seed fixed at: {SEED}\")\nprint(f\"Cross-validation folds: {N_FOLDS}\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-09T13:53:06.029023Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-09T13:53:06.029289Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-09T13:53:06.057453Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-09T13:53:06.029268Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-09T13:53:06.056543Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"---\n## 3. Data Loading and Inspection\n\n### Why this step matters\nBefore we can do *anything*, we need to load the three CSV files that Kaggle provides:\n1. **train.csv** - labelled data (we know who churned). We learn from this.\n2. **test.csv** - unlabelled data. We must predict churn probability for these customers.\n3. **sample_submission.csv** - shows the exact format Kaggle expects when we upload our predictions.\n\nWe will peek at the first few rows (`.head()`), check the shape (rows × columns), and inspect data types (`.info()`).","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Load competition data\ntrain = pd.read_csv('/kaggle/input/competitions/playground-series-s6e3/train.csv')\ntest  = pd.read_csv('/kaggle/input/competitions/playground-series-s6e3/test.csv')\nsample_sub   = pd.read_csv('/kaggle/input/competitions/playground-series-s6e3/sample_submission.csv')\n\nprint(f\"Training set:    {train.shape[0]:,} rows × {train.shape[1]} columns\")\nprint(f\"Test set:        {test.shape[0]:,} rows × {test.shape[1]} columns\")\nprint(f\"Submission file: {sample_sub.shape[0]:,} rows × {sample_sub.shape[1]} columns\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-09T13:53:06.059136Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-09T13:53:06.059463Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-09T13:53:07.486799Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-09T13:53:06.059433Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-09T13:53:07.485963Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# First 5 rows of training data\ntrain.head()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-09T13:53:07.487711Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-09T13:53:07.487974Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-09T13:53:07.501438Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-09T13:53:07.487950Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-09T13:53:07.500559Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"print(test.columns)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-09T13:53:07.503287Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-09T13:53:07.503554Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-09T13:53:07.523799Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-09T13:53:07.503532Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-09T13:53:07.522876Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Understanding the columns\n\n| Column | Type | Description |\n|--------|------|-------------|\n| **id** | Numeric | Unique customer identifier |\n| **gender** | Categorical | Male / Female |\n| **SeniorCitizen** | Binary (0/1) | Whether the customer is a senior citizen |\n| **Partner** | Categorical | Has a partner (Yes/No) |\n| **Dependents** | Categorical | Has dependents (Yes/No) |\n| **tenure** | Numeric | Number of months the customer has been with the company |\n| **PhoneService** | Categorical | Has phone service (Yes/No) |\n| **MultipleLines** | Categorical | Multiple phone lines (Yes/No/No phone service) |\n| **InternetService** | Categorical | Type of internet (DSL / Fiber optic / No) |\n| **OnlineSecurity – StreamingMovies** | Categorical | Various add-on services |\n| **Contract** | Categorical | Contract type (Month-to-month / One year / Two year) |\n| **PaperlessBilling** | Categorical | Paperless billing (Yes/No) |\n| **PaymentMethod** | Categorical | How the customer pays |\n| **MonthlyCharges** | Numeric | Monthly bill amount |\n| **TotalCharges** | Numeric | Total amount billed to date |\n| **Churn** | Target | Did the customer leave? (Yes/No) - **only in train.csv** |\n\n**Categorical features** hold text labels (e.g. \"Male\", \"DSL\").  \n**Numerical features** hold numbers (e.g. tenure = 29, MonthlyCharges = 60.1).\n\nML models work with numbers, so we will need to convert categorical features later.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"---\n## 4: Target Variable Analysis\n\n### Why analyse the target first?\nThe **Churn** column is what we are trying to predict. Before building any model we need to know:\n1. What values it contains (Yes / No)\n2. How balanced the classes are\n\nIf one class heavily outnumbers the other (**class imbalance**), a lazy model could just predict the majority class every time and still look accurate - but it would be useless. This is exactly why ROC-AUC is a better metric than simple accuracy.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Convert target to numeric: Yes -> 1, No -> 0\ntrain[\"Churn\"] = train[\"Churn\"].map({\"Yes\": 1, \"No\": 0})\n\n# Value counts\nchurn_counts = train[\"Churn\"].value_counts()\nprint(\"Churn distribution:\")\nprint(churn_counts)\nprint(f\"\\nChurn rate: {train['Churn'].mean()*100:.1f}%\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-09T13:53:07.524704Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-09T13:53:07.524893Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-09T13:53:07.568195Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-09T13:53:07.524876Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-09T13:53:07.567399Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Visualise class balance\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(6, 4))\nchurn_counts.plot(kind=\"bar\", color=[\"steelblue\", \"salmon\"], edgecolor=\"black\", ax=ax)\nax.set_xticklabels([\"No Churn (0)\", \"Churn (1)\"], rotation=0)\nax.set_ylabel(\"Number of Customers\")\nax.set_title(\"Target Variable Distribution\")\nfor i, v in enumerate(churn_counts):\n    ax.text(i, v + train.shape[0]*0.005, f\"{v:,}\", ha=\"center\", fontweight=\"bold\")\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-09T13:53:07.569020Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-09T13:53:07.569240Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-09T13:53:07.672150Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-09T13:53:07.569222Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-09T13:53:07.671385Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Takeaway\nThe dataset is **imbalanced** - there are significantly more non-churners than churners. This confirms that ROC-AUC is the right evaluation metric: it rewards a model that correctly ranks churners higher than non-churners, regardless of the threshold chosen.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"---\n## 5: Data Quality Check\n\n### Why check data quality?\nReal-world data is messy. Common problems include:\n- **Missing values** - some cells are empty. Most ML models cannot handle NaN.\n- **Duplicate rows** - repeated entries can bias the model.\n- **Wrong data types** - a number stored as text (e.g. TotalCharges) will not be computed correctly.\n- **Invalid values** - negative tenure, nonsensical categories, etc.\n\nWe will systematically inspect each issue and decide how to fix it.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# ---- Missing Values ----\nprint(\"=\" * 50)\nprint(\"MISSING VALUES\")\nprint(\"=\" * 50)\nmissing_train = train.isnull().sum()\nmissing_test  = test.isnull().sum()\n\nprint(\"\\nTraining set:\")\nprint(missing_train[missing_train > 0] if missing_train.sum() > 0 else \"No missing values!\")\nprint(\"\\nTest set:\")\nprint(missing_test[missing_test > 0] if missing_test.sum() > 0 else \"No missing values!\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-09T13:53:07.673065Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-09T13:53:07.673284Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-09T13:53:08.117961Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-09T13:53:07.673265Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-09T13:53:08.117287Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# ---- Check TotalCharges for hidden non-numeric values ----\n# TotalCharges is sometimes stored as text with blank strings instead of NaN\nprint(\"TotalCharges dtype (train):\", train[\"TotalCharges\"].dtype)\n\n# Force numeric conversion - invalid entries become NaN\ntrain[\"TotalCharges\"] = pd.to_numeric(train[\"TotalCharges\"], errors=\"coerce\")\ntest[\"TotalCharges\"]  = pd.to_numeric(test[\"TotalCharges\"],  errors=\"coerce\")\n\nnew_missing_train = train[\"TotalCharges\"].isnull().sum()\nnew_missing_test  = test[\"TotalCharges\"].isnull().sum()\nprint(f\"TotalCharges missing after conversion - train: {new_missing_train}, test: {new_missing_test}\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-09T13:53:08.118793Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-09T13:53:08.119003Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-09T13:53:08.127345Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-09T13:53:08.118985Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-09T13:53:08.126603Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# ---- Handle missing TotalCharges ----\n# Strategy: fill with tenure * MonthlyCharges (logical approximation)\nif new_missing_train > 0 or new_missing_test > 0:\n    train[\"TotalCharges\"].fillna(train[\"tenure\"] * train[\"MonthlyCharges\"], inplace=True)\n    test[\"TotalCharges\"].fillna(test[\"tenure\"] * test[\"MonthlyCharges\"], inplace=True)\n    print(\"Filled missing TotalCharges with tenure x MonthlyCharges.\")\nelse:\n    print(\"No missing TotalCharges - nothing to fill.\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-09T13:53:08.128119Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-09T13:53:08.128335Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-09T13:53:08.146786Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-09T13:53:08.128313Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-09T13:53:08.145961Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# ---- Duplicate Rows ----\nprint(f\"Duplicate rows in train: {train.duplicated().sum()}\")\nprint(f\"Duplicate rows in test:  {test.duplicated().sum()}\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-09T13:53:08.149729Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-09T13:53:08.149984Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-09T13:53:08.720846Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-09T13:53:08.149966Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-09T13:53:08.719961Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# ---- Quick sanity check on numeric ranges ----\nprint(train[[\"tenure\", \"MonthlyCharges\", \"TotalCharges\"]].describe())","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-09T13:53:08.721792Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-09T13:53:08.722030Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-09T13:53:08.786561Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-09T13:53:08.722009Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-09T13:53:08.785473Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"---\n## 6: Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)\n\n### Why explore the data visually?\nCharts let us spot patterns that raw numbers hide. We are looking for features that behave **differently** for churners vs. non-churners - those features will be the most useful predictors.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# ---- Numeric feature distributions by churn ----\nnumeric_cols = [\"tenure\", \"MonthlyCharges\", \"TotalCharges\"]\n\nfig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 3, figsize=(16, 4))\nfor i, col in enumerate(numeric_cols):\n    for label, color in [(0, \"green\"), (1, \"red\")]:\n        subset = train[train[\"Churn\"] == label][col]\n        axes[i].hist(subset, bins=40, alpha=0.6, label=f\"Churn={label}\", color=color, edgecolor=\"white\")\n    axes[i].set_title(col)\n    axes[i].legend()\nplt.suptitle(\"Numeric Feature Distributions by Churn\", y=1.02, fontsize=14)\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-09T13:53:08.787564Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-09T13:53:08.787855Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-09T13:53:09.937624Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-09T13:53:08.787827Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-09T13:53:09.936645Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Observations:**\n- **Tenure**: Customers who churn tend to have *shorter* tenures - they leave early.\n- **MonthlyCharges**: Churners tend to have *higher* monthly bills.\n- **TotalCharges**: Churners have *lower* total charges (because they haven't stayed long).\n\nThese are strong signals the model can learn from!","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# ---- Categorical features vs Churn ----\ncat_cols = [\"Contract\", \"InternetService\", \"PaymentMethod\", \"PaperlessBilling\"]\n\nfig, axes = plt.subplots(2, 2, figsize=(10, 10))\nfor idx, col in enumerate(cat_cols):\n    ax = axes[idx // 2][idx % 2]\n    ct = pd.crosstab(train[col], train[\"Churn\"], normalize=\"index\") * 100\n    ct.plot(kind=\"bar\", stacked=True, ax=ax, color=[\"green\", \"red\"], edgecolor=\"black\")\n    ax.set_title(f\"Churn Rate by {col}\")\n    ax.set_ylabel(\"Percentage\")\n    ax.legend([\"No Churn\", \"Churn\"], loc=\"upper right\")\n    ax.set_xticklabels(ax.get_xticklabels(), rotation=30, ha=\"right\")\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-09T13:53:09.938863Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-09T13:53:09.939168Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-09T13:53:10.541601Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-09T13:53:09.939145Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-09T13:53:10.540854Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Key insights:**\n- **Month-to-month** contracts have the highest churn rate - no commitment means easy to leave.\n- **Fiber optic** internet users churn more - possibly due to higher prices or service issues.\n- **Electronic check** payment is associated with higher churn.\n- **Paperless billing** users churn more often.\n\nThese patterns will guide our feature engineering.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# ---- Correlation heatmap for numeric features ----\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(8, 6))\ncorr = train[[\"tenure\", \"MonthlyCharges\", \"TotalCharges\", \"SeniorCitizen\", \"Churn\"]].corr()\nsns.heatmap(corr, annot=True, fmt=\".2f\", cmap=\"coolwarm\", center=0, ax=ax)\nax.set_title(\"Correlation Matrix\")\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-09T13:53:10.542559Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-09T13:53:10.542799Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-09T13:53:10.764595Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-09T13:53:10.542779Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-09T13:53:10.763890Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### What This Correlation Matrix Is showing\n\nA correlation value goes from **-1 to +1**:\n- **Close to +1** (dark red) = when one goes up, the other goes up too\n- **Close to -1** (dark blue) = when one goes up, the other goes down\n- **Close to 0** (white/light) = no real relationship\n\nHere's what each number means in plain English:\n\n**tenure → Churn = -0.42** (blue)\nCustomers who have been with the company *longer* are *less likely* to churn. Loyalty protects retention. This is my **strongest signal**.\n\n**MonthlyCharges → Churn = +0.27** (light red)\nCustomers with *higher bills* are *more likely* to churn. Expensive plans push people away.\n\n**TotalCharges → Churn = -0.22** (light blue)\nHigher total charges means the customer has stayed longer and paid more overall so they're *slightly less likely* to churn.\n\n**SeniorCitizen → Churn = +0.24** (light red)\nSenior citizens churn a bit more than younger customers.\n\n**tenure → TotalCharges = +0.77** (strong red)\nThis just makes logical sense - the longer you stay, the more total money you've paid.\n\n**The big takeaway for my model:**\n> - **Tenure** is my most powerful predictor of churn. Long-tenured customers almost never leave.\n> - **MonthlyCharges** is the second most important - high bills drive customers away.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"---\n## 7: Feature Engineering\n\n### Why create new features?\nRaw columns sometimes don't capture the full story. By **combining** or **transforming** existing features we can give the model richer information.\n\n| New Feature | Logic | Why it might help |\n|-------------|-------|-------------------|\n| **tenure_group** | Bucket tenure into groups (0-12, 13-24, etc.) | Captures non-linear effects of loyalty |\n| **service_count** | Count how many services a customer subscribes to | More services = more locked in = less likely to churn? |\n| **avg_monthly_charge** | TotalCharges / (tenure + 1) | Smooths out billing fluctuations |\n| **charge_per_service** | MonthlyCharges / (service_count + 1) | Cost efficiency per service |","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"categorical_columns = train.select_dtypes(include=\"object\").columns\nfor col in categorical_columns:\n    print(col)\n    print(train[col].unique())","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-09T13:53:10.765538Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-09T13:53:10.765805Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-09T13:53:11.143457Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-09T13:53:10.765781Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-09T13:53:11.142647Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Apply feature engineering to both train and test\nfor df in [train, test]:\n    # Tenure groups\n    df[\"tenure_group\"] = pd.cut(df[\"tenure\"], bins=[0, 12, 24, 36, 48, 60, 72],\n                                 labels=[\"0-12\", \"13-24\", \"25-36\", \"37-48\", \"49-60\", \"61-72\"])\n\n    # Count of subscribed services\n    service_cols = [\"PhoneService\", \"MultipleLines\", \"InternetService\",\n                    \"OnlineSecurity\", \"OnlineBackup\", \"DeviceProtection\",\n                    \"TechSupport\", \"StreamingTV\", \"StreamingMovies\"]\n    df[\"service_count\"] = df[service_cols].apply(\n        lambda row: sum(1 for v in row if v not in [\"No\", \"No internet service\", \"No phone service\"]),\n        axis=1\n    )\n\n    # Average monthly charge\n    df[\"avg_monthly_charge\"] = df[\"TotalCharges\"] / (df[\"tenure\"] + 1)\n\n    # Charge per service\n    df[\"charge_per_service\"] = df[\"MonthlyCharges\"] / (df[\"service_count\"] + 1)\n\nprint(\"New features created!\")\nprint(\"Columns now:\", train.shape[1])\ntrain[[\"tenure\", \"TotalCharges\", \"MonthlyCharges\", \"tenure_group\", \"service_count\", \"avg_monthly_charge\", \"charge_per_service\"]].head(10)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-09T13:53:11.145005Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-09T13:53:11.145335Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-09T13:53:14.428662Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-09T13:53:11.145310Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-09T13:53:14.427876Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"---\n\n## 8: Encoding Categorical Variables\n\n### Why encode?\nML models understand numbers, not text. We must convert every categorical column to a numeric representation.\n\n**Two main approaches:**\n\n| Method | How it works | Best for |\n|--------|-------------|----------|\n| **Label Encoding** | Assigns an integer to each category (Male=0, Female=1) | Binary or ordinal categories |\n| **One-Hot Encoding** | Creates a new 0/1 column for each category | Nominal (no order) categories with few unique values |\n\nFor this dataset we will use **Label Encoding** for all categorical columns. It is simpler, works well with tree-based models (Random Forest, XGBoost), and keeps the number of columns manageable.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Identify categorical columns (object dtype) in training data\n# Exclude \"id\" and \"Churn\" (target)\ncat_columns = train.select_dtypes(include=\"object\").columns.tolist()\nprint(f\"Categorical columns to encode ({len(cat_columns)}): {cat_columns}\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-09T13:53:14.429530Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-09T13:53:14.429716Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-09T13:53:14.500123Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-09T13:53:14.429699Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-09T13:53:14.498880Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Label-encode each categorical column\nlabel_encoders = {}\n\nfor col in cat_columns:\n    le = LabelEncoder()\n    # Fit on combined train+test to ensure consistent encoding\n    combined_values = pd.concat([train[col], test[col]], axis=0).astype(str)\n    le.fit(combined_values)\n    train[col] = le.transform(train[col].astype(str))\n    test[col]  = le.transform(test[col].astype(str))\n    label_encoders[col] = le\n\nprint(\"All categorical columns encoded!\")\ntrain.head()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-09T13:53:14.501180Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-09T13:53:14.501449Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-09T13:53:16.150678Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-09T13:53:14.501425Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-09T13:53:16.149571Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Also encode tenure_group (it was created as a categorical)\nle_tg = LabelEncoder()\ncombined_tg = pd.concat([train[\"tenure_group\"].astype(str), test[\"tenure_group\"].astype(str)])\nle_tg.fit(combined_tg)\ntrain[\"tenure_group\"] = le_tg.transform(train[\"tenure_group\"].astype(str))\ntest[\"tenure_group\"]  = le_tg.transform(test[\"tenure_group\"].astype(str))\nprint(\"tenure_group encoded.\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-09T13:53:16.151581Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-09T13:53:16.151795Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-09T13:53:16.400495Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-09T13:53:16.151775Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-09T13:53:16.399439Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"---\n\n## 9: Feature Scaling\n\n### Why scale features?\nSome models (e.g. Logistic Regression) are sensitive to the **magnitude** of features. If `tenure` ranges 0–72 but `TotalCharges` ranges 0–9000, the model might give unfair weight to TotalCharges simply because its numbers are bigger.\n\n| Method | Formula | When to use |\n|--------|---------|-------------|\n| **Standardisation (Z-score)** | (x − mean) / std | Most general-purpose; centres data around 0 |\n| **Min-Max Normalisation** | (x − min) / (max − min) | When you need values strictly in [0, 1] |\n\nTree-based models (Random Forest, XGBoost) are **not** affected by scale, but Logistic Regression **is**. We will scale features for all models to keep things consistent.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Separate target and features\ntarget = train[\"Churn\"]\ndrop_cols = [\"id\", \"Churn\"]\nfeature_cols = [c for c in train.columns if c not in drop_cols]\n\nX = train[feature_cols].copy()\nX_test = test[feature_cols].copy()\n\nprint(f\"Feature matrix shape: {X.shape}\")\nprint(f\"Test matrix shape:    {X_test.shape}\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-09T13:53:16.401451Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-09T13:53:16.401673Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-09T13:53:16.548786Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-09T13:53:16.401653Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-09T13:53:16.548100Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"X.head()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-09T13:53:16.549702Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-09T13:53:16.549927Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-09T13:53:16.561664Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-09T13:53:16.549909Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-09T13:53:16.560882Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Apply StandardScaler\nscaler = StandardScaler()\nX_scaled = pd.DataFrame(scaler.fit_transform(X), columns=feature_cols, index=X.index)\nX_test_scaled = pd.DataFrame(scaler.transform(X_test), columns=feature_cols, index=X_test.index)\n\nprint(\"Scaling complete. Sample:\")\nX_scaled.head()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-09T13:53:16.562661Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-09T13:53:16.562919Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-09T13:53:16.770972Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-09T13:53:16.562894Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-09T13:53:16.770028Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"---\n\n### StandardScaler\n\nIn my churn dataset, every column is called a **feature**. Things like:\n- How long the customer stayed → **tenure**\n- How much they pay monthly → **MonthlyCharges**\n- How much they paid in total → **TotalCharges**\n\nImagine you are hiring a chef. You judge them on two things:\n- **Years of experience** → ranges from 1 to 10\n- **Number of recipes they know** → ranges from 100 to 5000\n  \nNow you ask my friend to score both and pick the better chef. my friend looks at the numbers and says:\n*\"Chef A knows 4000 recipes and has 5 years experience. Chef B knows 500 recipes and has 9 years experience. Chef A wins obviously - 4000 is way bigger than 500!\"*\n\nBut wait. **9 years of experience is actually incredible.** my friend was fooled by the big recipe number. They compared 4000 vs 500 instead of fairly judging both categories.\n\n**That is exactly what happens to Logistic Regression without StandardScaler.**\n\n### Now With my Actual Churn Data\n\n| Customer | tenure | TotalCharges |\n|----------|--------|--------------|\n| Sneha    | 2      | 170          |\n| Sanskar  | 72     | 8500         |\n\nThe model does this math to calculate churn risk:\n```\nWithout Scaling:\nSneha churn score = tenure(2)  +  TotalCharges(170)  =  172\nSanskar   churn score = tenure(72) +  TotalCharges(8500) =  8572\n```\nThe model basically **only sees TotalCharges** because 8500 dominates everything. Tenure is invisible. It contributes only 2 or 72 - tiny compared to thousands.\nBut we KNOW from the correlation matrix that **tenure is my strongest churn predictor** with -0.42 correlation. Short tenure = very likely to churn. The model is ignoring my best weapon.\n\n### What StandardScaler Does Step by Step\nIt takes each column and applies this simple idea:\n> *\"How far is this value from the average? Express that distance in standard units.\"*\n\nThe formula is just:\n```\nnew value = (my value - average of column) / spread of column\n```\nAfter doing this, every single column ends up with:\n- Average = **0**\n- All values roughly between **-3 and +3**\n\n### my Data Before and After Scaling\n**Before scaling - completely unfair:**\n| Customer | tenure | TotalCharges |\n|----------|--------|-------------|\n| Sneha | 2 | 170 |\n|Sanskar | 72 | 8500 |\n| Sakshi | 24 | 3200 |\n\n**After scaling - perfectly fair:**\n| Customer | tenure | TotalCharges |\n|----------|--------|-------------|\n| Sneha | -1.30 | -1.20 |\n| Sanskar | 1.50 | 1.40 |\n| Sakshi| -0.20 | 0.10 |\n\nNow both columns live between -2 and +2. The model treats them **equally and fairly.**\n\n### What Happens If You Skip It\n| Situation | What Happens |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| You use **Random Forest** | Nothing bad - trees don't care about scale ✅ |\n| You use **XGBoost** | Nothing bad - same reason ✅ |\n| You use **Logistic Regression** | Quietly gives you wrong predictions ❌ |\n\nThe scary part is **no error message appears.** The code runs fine. The predictions come out. But they are silently wrong because the model was judging features unfairly the whole time. my Kaggle ROC-AUC score will be lower than it should be and you will have no idea why.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"---\n## 10: Train / Validation Split\n\n### Why split the data?\nIf we train and evaluate on the **same** data, the model can just memorise answers this is called **overfitting**. It will look great on training data but fail on new customers.\n\nBy holding out a portion as a **validation set**, we simulate Kaggle's hidden test and get an honest estimate of my model's performance.\n\nWe use an 80/20 split and **stratify** by the target to ensure both halves have the same churn ratio.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"X_train, X_val, y_train, y_val = train_test_split(\n    X_scaled,        # my features (all columns except Churn)\n    target,          # the answer column (Churn = 0 or 1)\n    test_size=0.20,  # keep 20% aside for testing\n    random_state=42, # so you get same split every time you run\n    stratify=target  # explained below\n)\n\nprint(f\"Training:   {X_train.shape[0]:,} samples\")\nprint(f\"Validation: {X_val.shape[0]:,} samples\")\nprint(f\"Churn rate - train: {y_train.mean():.3f}, val: {y_val.mean():.3f}\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-09T13:53:16.771769Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-09T13:53:16.771947Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-09T13:53:17.046202Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-09T13:53:16.771930Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-09T13:53:17.045409Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Visualising The Split\n```\nmy 594,000 customers\n├── 80% → X_train, y_train  (475,000 customers) → MODEL LEARNS FROM THIS\n└── 20% → X_val,   y_val    (119,000 customers) → MODEL IS TESTED ON THIS\n```\nThe model **never sees** the validation set during learning. It is a fair blind test.\n\n---\n\n### What Is `stratify=target`?\nThis is very important because my churn data is **imbalanced** - roughly 73% No Churn and 27% Churn.\nWithout stratify, by bad luck you might get:\n```\nTraining set   → 80% No Churn, 20% Churn\nValidation set → 60% No Churn, 40% Churn   ← completely different ratio, unfair test\n```\nWith `stratify=target`, it **guarantees** both sets have the same ratio:\n```\nTraining set   → 73% No Churn, 27% Churn\nValidation set → 73% No Churn, 27% Churn\n```\nThe last print line confirms this worked:\n```\nChurn rate - train: 0.265, val: 0.265  ← identical, perfect!","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"---\n## 11: Train Multiple Models","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"\n## Model 1. Logistic Regression\n\n### What It Does\n\nImagine plotting every customer on a graph. Blue dots = stayed, Red dots = churned.\n\n```\nMonthly Charges\n     |        🔴 🔴 🔴\n     |     🔴 🔴 🔴\n     |___________← draws this straight line\n     |  🔵 🔵 \n     | 🔵 🔵 🔵\n     |_________________ Tenure\n```\n\nLogistic Regression simply **draws one straight line** to separate churners from non-churners. Everyone on the left side = probably stays. Everyone on the right side = probably churns.\n\n### Real Life Analogy\nThink of a bank deciding who gets a loan. They say:\n> *\"If income is above $50,000 AND age is above 25 → approve. Otherwise → reject.\"*\n\nThat is a straight line decision. Simple, fast, explainable.\n\n### For Churn It Learns Things Like\n> *\"If tenure is low AND monthly charges are high → likely to churn\"*\n\n### Strengths and Weaknesses\n\n| Strengths | Weaknesses |\n|-----------|-----------|\n| Very fast to train | Cannot learn curved/complex patterns |\n| Easy to explain to business | Needs feature scaling |\n| Great starting baseline | Struggles with many interacting features |\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Dictionary to store results\nresults = {}\n\n# ---- 1. Logistic Regression ----\nprint(\"Training Logistic Regression...\")\nlr = LogisticRegression(max_iter=1000, random_state=42)\nlr.fit(X_train, y_train)\nlr_proba = lr.predict_proba(X_val)[:, 1]\nlr_auc = roc_auc_score(y_val, lr_proba)\nresults[\"Logistic Regression\"] = lr_auc\nprint(f\"  ROC-AUC: {lr_auc:.4f}\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-09T13:53:17.047290Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-09T13:53:17.047508Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-09T13:53:21.949531Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-09T13:53:17.047487Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-09T13:53:21.948978Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"---\n\n## Model 2. Random Forest\n\n### What It Does - Start With One Decision Tree\n\nA single decision tree asks questions like a flowchart:\n\n```\nIs tenure < 12 months?\n        │\n   YES──┘──NO\n   │         │\nHigh churn   Is MonthlyCharges > $70?\nrisk 🔴            │\n              YES──┘──NO\n              │         │\n         High churn   Low churn\n         risk 🔴      risk 🔵\n```\n\nOne tree is okay but makes mistakes. So Random Forest builds **300 trees** - each tree sees a random subset of customers and random subset of features.\n\n### The Voting System\n\n```\nCustomer Sneha enters:\n  tenure=2, MonthlyCharges=85, Contract=Month-to-month\n\nTree 1   → Churn ✅\nTree 2   → Churn ✅\nTree 3   → No Churn ❌\nTree 4   → Churn ✅\nTree 5   → Churn ✅\n...\nTree 300 → Churn ✅\n\nFinal Vote: 258 say Churn, 42 say No Churn\nAnswer: 0.86 churn probability 🔴\n```\n\n### Real Life Analogy\nYou want to know which restaurant to go to. You ask **300 different friends** instead of just one. You go with what the majority says. Much more reliable than trusting one person.\n\n### For Churn It Learns Things Like\n> *\"Tree 47 notices that month-to-month contract + fiber optic + short tenure = almost always churns\"*\n> *\"Tree 183 notices that senior citizens with high bills churn a lot\"*\n> *\"Together they catch patterns no single tree would find alone\"*\n\n### Strengths and Weaknesses\n\n| Strengths | Weaknesses |\n|-----------|-----------|\n| Very accurate | Slower than Logistic Regression |\n| Handles complex patterns | Hard to explain individual decisions |\n| Does not need scaling | Uses more memory |\n| Resistant to overfitting | |\n\n---","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# ---- 2. Random Forest ----\nprint(\"Training Random Forest...\")\nrf = RandomForestClassifier(n_estimators=300, max_depth=10, random_state=42, n_jobs=-1)\nrf.fit(X_train, y_train)\nrf_proba = rf.predict_proba(X_val)[:, 1]\nrf_auc = roc_auc_score(y_val, rf_proba)\nresults[\"Random Forest\"] = rf_auc\nprint(f\"  ROC-AUC: {rf_auc:.4f}\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-09T13:53:21.950182Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-09T13:53:21.950371Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-09T13:54:27.961737Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-09T13:53:21.950353Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-09T13:54:27.960437Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Model 3. Gradient Boosting\n\n### What It Does\n\nThis is where it gets clever. Instead of 300 independent trees voting, Gradient Boosting builds trees **one by one** - and each new tree specifically focuses on fixing what the previous tree got wrong.\n\n### Step By Step\n\n```\nStep 1: Tree 1 makes predictions\n        Got Sneha right ✅  Got Sanskar wrong ❌  Got Sakshi right ✅\n\nStep 2: Tree 2 looks at mistakes\n        \"Sanskar was wrong - let me focus extra hard on customers like Sanskar\"\n        Got Sanskar right ✅  Got someone else wrong ❌\n\nStep 3: Tree 3 looks at NEW mistakes\n        \"Let me fix the new mistakes...\"\n\n...repeat 300 times...\n\nFinal: All 300 trees combined = very accurate model\n```\n\n### Real Life Analogy\n\nThink of a student taking practice exams:\n- **Exam 1** → scores 60%. Reviews wrong answers.\n- **Exam 2** → focuses on weak topics. Scores 72%. Reviews wrong answers again.\n- **Exam 3** → focuses on remaining weak spots. Scores 81%.\n- After 300 practice exams → becomes an expert.\n\nEach attempt specifically targets previous failures. That is **Gradient Boosting**.\n\n### For Churn It Learns Things Like\n> *\"First tree gets easy churners right - obvious cases like 1 month tenure, $90 bill\"*\n> *\"Second tree focuses on tricky cases - maybe senior citizens with long tenure but high bills\"*\n> *\"By tree 300 it is catching very subtle patterns\"*\n\n### Strengths and Weaknesses\n\n| Strengths | Weaknesses |\n|-----------|-----------|\n| Very high accuracy | Slow to train |\n| Catches subtle patterns | Can overfit if not careful |\n| Great for imbalanced data | Many settings to tune |\n\n---\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# # ---- 3. Gradient Boosting ----\n# print(\"Training Gradient Boosting...\")\n# gb = GradientBoostingClassifier(n_estimators=300, learning_rate=0.05, max_depth=4, random_state=42)\n# gb.fit(X_train, y_train)\n# gb_proba = gb.predict_proba(X_val)[:, 1]\n# gb_auc = roc_auc_score(y_val, gb_proba)\n# results[\"Gradient Boosting\"] = gb_auc\n# print(f\"  ROC-AUC: {gb_auc:.4f}\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-09T13:54:27.962952Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-09T13:54:27.963230Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-09T13:54:27.967030Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-09T13:54:27.963207Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-09T13:54:27.966183Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Model 4. XGBoost\n\n### What It Does\n\nXGBoost is basically Gradient Boosting with several smart improvements added on top. The X stands for **eXtreme** - it is an extreme version of Gradient Boosting.\n\n### What Makes It Different\n\n| Feature | Regular Gradient Boosting | XGBoost |\n|---------|--------------------------|---------|\n| Speed | Slow | **10x faster** |\n| Overfitting protection | Basic | **Built-in regularisation** |\n| Missing values | You must handle them | **Handles automatically** |\n| Parallel processing | No | **Uses all CPU cores** |\n\n### The Regularisation Part Simply Explained\n\nRegular Gradient Boosting is like a student who **memorises** every single practice exam answer including weird edge cases. XGBoost adds a penalty:\n\n> *\"Do not make my trees too complicated. Keep them simple and general.\"*\n\nThis means XGBoost generalises better to new unseen customers - exactly what you need for Kaggle test data.\n\n### Why XGBoost Wins on Kaggle\n\nBetween 2015 and 2023, XGBoost won **more Kaggle competitions than any other single algorithm.** For churn prediction specifically it works brilliantly because:\n\n- Churn data is **imbalanced** → XGBoost handles this well\n- Churn has **complex interactions** between features → XGBoost catches them\n- Churn dataset is **large** → XGBoost is fast enough to handle it","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# ---- 4. XGBoost ----\nif XGBOOST_AVAILABLE:\n    print(\"Training XGBoost...\")\n    xgb = XGBClassifier(\n        n_estimators=300, learning_rate=0.05, max_depth=5,\n        eval_metric=\"auc\", use_label_encoder=False,\n        random_state=42, n_jobs=-1\n    )\n    xgb.fit(X_train, y_train, eval_set=[(X_val, y_val)], verbose=False)\n    xgb_proba = xgb.predict_proba(X_val)[:, 1]\n    xgb_auc = roc_auc_score(y_val, xgb_proba)\n    results[\"XGBoost\"] = xgb_auc\n    print(f\"  ROC-AUC: {xgb_auc:.4f}\")\nelse:\n    print(\"XGBoost not available - skipped.\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-09T13:54:27.967977Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-09T13:54:27.968223Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-09T13:54:36.877782Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-09T13:54:27.968204Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-09T13:54:36.876735Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 12: Model Evaluation\n\n### Why compare models this way?\nDifferent algorithms make different trade-offs. We compare them using:\n1. **ROC-AUC score** - my primary metric.\n2. **ROC curves** - visual comparison of True Positive Rate vs False Positive Rate.\n3. **Confusion matrix** - for the best model, shows how many predictions were right/wrong.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# ---- Comparison table ----\nresults_df = pd.DataFrame.from_dict(results, orient=\"index\", columns=[\"ROC-AUC\"])\nresults_df = results_df.sort_values(\"ROC-AUC\", ascending=False)\nprint(\"\\n\" + \"=\" * 40)\nprint(\"MODEL COMPARISON\")\nprint(\"=\" * 40)\nprint(results_df.to_string())\n\nbest_model_name = results_df.index[0]\nprint(f\"\\nBest model: {best_model_name} (ROC-AUC = {results_df.iloc[0, 0]:.4f})\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-09T13:54:36.880658Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-09T13:54:36.880848Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-09T13:54:36.888025Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-09T13:54:36.880833Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-09T13:54:36.887110Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Imagine you have my 119,000 validation customers sitting in front of you. You randomly pick one customer who actually churned and one customer who actually stayed. You show both to my XGBoost model and ask \"which one of these two is more likely to leave?\" The model looks at their tenure, monthly charges, contract type, and all other features then gives each one a probability score. If the churner gets a higher score than the loyal customer, that is a correct answer. If somehow the loyal customer gets a higher score, that is a wrong answer. Now you repeat this game 100 times picking 100 different random pairs. 0.9159 means XGBoost gives the correct answer 91 times and gets it wrong only 9 times. That is genuinely excellent it means the model has truly learned who is risky and who is not.\n\nNow look at all four models doing the same game with 100 random pairs XGBoost gets it right 91.59 times, Gradient Boosting gets it right 91.55 times, Random Forest gets it right 91.26 times, and Logistic Regression gets it right 90.69 times. The difference between best and worst is less than 1 correct answer out of 100 - incredibly close. But we still pick XGBoost as the winner because even that tiny edge matters when you are predicting for 254,000 real customers in test.csv. A 0.009 improvement in AUC across 254,000 customers means hundreds of churners correctly identified that would have been missed otherwise and in a real telecom business, each saved customer is worth hundreds of dollars.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# ---- ROC Curves ----\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(8, 6))\n\nmodel_probas = {\n    \"Logistic Regression\": lr_proba,\n    \"Random Forest\": rf_proba,\n    # \"Gradient Boosting\": gb_proba,\n}\nif XGBOOST_AVAILABLE:\n    model_probas[\"XGBoost\"] = xgb_proba\n\nfor name, proba in model_probas.items():\n    fpr, tpr, _ = roc_curve(y_val, proba)\n    ax.plot(fpr, tpr, label=f\"{name} (AUC={results[name]:.4f})\")\n\nax.plot([0, 1], [0, 1], \"k--\", alpha=0.4, label=\"Random Guess\")\nax.set_xlabel(\"False Positive Rate\")\nax.set_ylabel(\"True Positive Rate\")\nax.set_title(\"ROC Curves - Model Comparison\")\nax.legend(loc=\"lower right\")\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-09T13:54:36.888919Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-09T13:54:36.889163Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-09T13:54:37.117025Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-09T13:54:36.889144Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-09T13:54:37.116136Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### The Complete Picture - From Training To Business Decision\n\nThe entire process starts with a simple goal: find customers who are about to leave before they actually do. my model looks at every customer and outputs a probability - a number between 0 and 1 that says how likely that customer is to churn. For example if the model says 0.85 for a customer, it means there is an 85% chance that customer is going to cancel their subscription. But outputting a probability is not enough - the business needs to decide what to do with it. So you pick a cut-off point, say 0.5, which means anyone above 0.5 gets flagged as a churner and the retention team calls them with a discount offer, and anyone below 0.5 gets ignored because they are probably staying. The problem is - why 0.5? What if 0.3 is better? What if 0.8 works more efficiently? Every cut-off gives completely different results and different types of mistakes. If you lower the cut-off to 0.3 you catch more real churners but waste calls on loyal customers. If you raise it to 0.8 you save on unnecessary calls but miss real churners who quietly leave. The ROC curve solves this by testing every single possible cut-off from 0.0 to 1.0 and plotting all the results on one chart, so you can see the full picture of my model's behaviour instead of being locked into one arbitrary threshold. The AUC number - 0.9159 for my XGBoost - summarises that entire curve into one score, meaning out of any random pair of one churner and one loyal customer, my model correctly identifies the churner as riskier 91.59 times out of 100.\n\nNow the important question - where are the actual business decisions made, and what is the 20% validation data actually for? The 20% validation split is purely my internal quality check. You held back 119,000 customers from train.csv whose churn outcome you already know, ran my model on them, and checked the AUC score of 0.9159 to confirm the model is trustworthy before using it on real unseen customers. Nobody outside my notebook ever sees this step - it is simply you making sure the model learned correctly before trusting it with real decisions. Once you are confident, you move to test.csv - the 254,000 real customers whose churn outcome is hidden even from you. The model scores every single one of them with a probability, and that is where the real business action happens. A customer with 0.91 probability gets called immediately by the retention team. A customer with 0.87 gets offered a discount today. A customer with 0.12 is left alone because they are clearly staying. The business sets a simple rule - anyone above 0.70 gets a retention call, anyone below 0.70 is safe - and my submission.csv with those probability scores is exactly what drives that decision for all 254,000 customers.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# ---- Confusion Matrix for the best model ----\n# Pick the best model object\nbest_models = {\"Logistic Regression\": lr, \"Random Forest\": rf}\n#\"Gradient Boosting\": gb}\nif XGBOOST_AVAILABLE:\n    best_models[\"XGBoost\"] = xgb\n\nbest_model = best_models[best_model_name]\nbest_proba = model_probas[best_model_name]\nbest_preds = (best_proba >= 0.5).astype(int)\n\ncm = confusion_matrix(y_val, best_preds)\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(5, 4))\nsns.heatmap(cm, annot=True, fmt=\",\", cmap=\"Blues\",\n            xticklabels=[\"Predicted Stay\", \"Predicted Churn\"],\n            yticklabels=[\"Actual Stay\", \"Actual Churn\"], ax=ax)\nax.set_title(f\"Confusion Matrix - {best_model_name}\")\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-09T13:56:15.453977Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-09T13:56:15.454281Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-09T13:56:15.595445Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-09T13:56:15.454261Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-09T13:56:15.594151Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"#### Confusion Matrix - XGBoost\n\nOut of 119,839 total validation customers, the model looked at each one and made a prediction. It correctly identified 85,025 loyal customers as staying and correctly caught 17,144 real churners those are the good predictions. But it made two types of mistakes: it wrongly flagged 7,051 loyal customers as churners (it called them risky but they were actually going to stay wasted discount calls), and more importantly it missed 9,619 real churners completely (it said they were safe but they actually left these are the most dangerous mistakes because the business lost these customers without ever trying to save them). In simple terms my XGBoost got the right answer for 102,169 customers out of 119,839, which is why the AUC is 0.9159 genuinely excellent, but those 9,619 missed churners are exactly what further model improvement would try to reduce.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 13: Train the Final Model on Full Training Data\n\n### Why retrain on all training data?\nSo far we held out 20% for validation. Now that we know which model performs best, we retrain it on **100%** of the training data so it has the maximum amount of information before predicting the test set.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Retrain best model on full training data\nprint(f\"Retraining {best_model_name} on full training data ({X_scaled.shape[0]:,} rows)...\")\n\nif best_model_name == \"Logistic Regression\":\n    final_model = LogisticRegression(max_iter=1000, random_state=42)\nelif best_model_name == \"Random Forest\":\n    final_model = RandomForestClassifier(n_estimators=300, max_depth=10, random_state=42, n_jobs=-1)\nelif best_model_name == \"Gradient Boosting\":\n    final_model = GradientBoostingClassifier(n_estimators=300, learning_rate=0.05, max_depth=4, random_state=42)\nelif best_model_name == \"XGBoost\":\n    final_model = XGBClassifier(n_estimators=300, learning_rate=0.05, max_depth=5,\n                                eval_metric=\"auc\", use_label_encoder=False, random_state=42, n_jobs=-1)\n\nfinal_model.fit(X_scaled, target)\nprint(\"Done!\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-09T13:56:20.642515Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-09T13:56:20.642802Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-09T13:56:27.607959Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-09T13:56:20.642780Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-09T13:56:27.607203Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 14: Predict on the Test Data\n\n### Why predict probabilities?\nKaggle evaluates us with ROC-AUC, which needs **probabilities** (values between 0 and 1), not hard class labels (0 or 1). We use `.predict_proba(X)[:, 1]` to get the probability of class 1 (Churn = Yes).","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"test_predictions = final_model.predict_proba(X_test_scaled)[:, 1]\n\nprint(f\"Predictions generated for {len(test_predictions):,} customers.\")\nprint(f\"Min probability:  {test_predictions.min():.4f}\")\nprint(f\"Max probability:  {test_predictions.max():.4f}\")\nprint(f\"Mean probability: {test_predictions.mean():.4f}\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-09T13:56:29.737463Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-09T13:56:29.737736Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-09T13:56:30.199780Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-09T13:56:29.737717Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-09T13:56:30.199337Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"XGBoost model just looked at all **254,655 customers** in test.csv - the real unseen customers whose churn outcome nobody knows - and gave every single one of them a churn probability score. That is all this code does. It runs through each customer, checks their tenure, monthly charges, contract type, and all other features, and outputs one number between 0 and 1 saying how risky that customer is.\n\nNow look at the three numbers it printed:\n\n**Min probability = 0.0003** means somewhere in those 254,655 customers there is one person who is extremely safe - the model is 99.97% sure they are staying. Probably someone with 6 years of tenure, a two year contract, low monthly bill, paying by bank transfer. Classic loyal customer profile.\n\n**Max probability = 0.9833** means somewhere there is one customer the model is 98.33% sure is about to leave. Probably joined last month, on a month to month contract, paying $90+ a month, using fiber optic, paying by electronic check. Every single warning sign is there.\n\n**Mean probability = 0.2183** means on average across all 254,655 customers the churn probability is 21.83%. This makes complete sense because in training data roughly 27% of customers churned - so the model is predicting a similar churn rate on the test data, which means it learned the real world pattern correctly and is not doing anything weird or broken.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 15: Create Submission File\n\n### How Kaggle evaluates this file\nI fill in the `Churn` column of `sample_submission.csv` with my predicted probabilities. Kaggle compares these against the true labels (which only they have) and computes the ROC-AUC score.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# ============================================================\n# SECTION 15 — Create Submission File\n# ============================================================\nimport os\n\ntry:\n    submission = sample_sub.copy()\n    submission[\"Churn\"] = test_predictions\n\n    # Verify row count matches exactly\n    assert len(submission) == len(sample_sub), \"Row count mismatch!\"\n    assert \"Churn\" in submission.columns, \"Churn column missing!\"\n    assert submission[\"Churn\"].isnull().sum() == 0, \"Null values in predictions!\"\n\n    # Simple save — exactly like all top scoring notebooks\n    submission.to_csv(\"submission.csv\", index=False)\n\n    print(\"Submission head:\")\n    print(submission.head(10))\n    print(f\"\\nShape: {submission.shape}\")\n    print(f\"File size: {os.path.getsize('submission.csv')/1024:.1f} KB\")\n    print(\"\\nSUCCESS — submission.csv saved!\")\n\nexcept Exception as e:\n    print(f\"ERROR: {e}\")\n    print(\"Saving sample submission as fallback...\")\n    sample_sub.to_csv(\"submission.csv\", index=False)\n    print(\"Fallback submission saved.\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-09T13:56:36.033256Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-09T13:56:36.033554Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-09T13:56:36.289990Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-09T13:56:36.033531Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-09T13:56:36.288965Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null}]}